foreign  [Music]  over the past two years we've been  working on this workflow that's been  helpful for us in processing large  amounts of data very quickly in order to  create products that help guide the  decision making in the state of where to  allocate resources this workflow  involves three main products Microsoft  Azure devops our studio workbench and  RSU connect if you're at the keynote  this morning you'll realize these some  of these terms are a little outdated  since we're now are you just changing to  pause it  when I first started working at cdph it  was December 2020. I was living in  Washington DC in Foggy Bottom so not too  far from here and if you remember in our  personal lives it was quite stressful so  going outside was kind of like rolling  the dice or whether you might get sick  or get others sick  and you can imagine working in public  health is also quite stressful our  infrastructure wasn't prepared for the  amount of data that Kobe was going to  provide and we're often trying to catch  up and get ahead  I happen to be working on a report at  that time that got sent out at the end  of the day of the day and I would get  this beautiful data set that was  processed by my many colleagues from the  local health jurisdictions and at the  state level and sometimes there would be  delays to no fault that anyone the  situation was very complicated data was  always changing so this often meant 9 pm  10 pm 11 pm California time midnight 1am  2 A.M and DC so I have to look like this  I was very tired and my cat was  wondering why I'm still awake  as a team we knew this wasn't feasible  and we needed to do something to change  this not only to meet the needs of the  state but also to improve our work-like  balance So eventually this workflow came  about allowing us to do our work more  efficiently and I got into bed at a more  reasonable time  so reducing our stress required four  main things the first one is how we  prioritize our task  how do we improve our ability to  collaborate with each other  how do we decrease our turnaround time  and then lastly how do we scale our work  so I'm going to start with or how do we  improve our prioritization so during  this time there are many requests always  coming in and there's often underturned  up I needed this yesterday and that's  very true because this the questions and  issues that we're having impacted the  health of millions of people  and the issue however is that as a team  we're quite limited there's only so many  of us our skills vary and we want to be  able to solve these issues but we only  have so much time as well so we needed  to wait to prioritize how we move  forward in our work  so that's where first product comes in  Microsoft Azure devops and we use  microsoftware develops at cdph because  we're a Microsoft Shop but you could use  other devop platforms as well in place  of this  so we started using Azure devops for  project management in particular a  Sprint board and a Sprint is a defined  period of time that you set to complete  a number of tasks and what's really  great about a Sprint is that at the very  beginning of the Sprint it forces you to  sit down and decide what what needs to  be done  what can realistically be done in this  time period and who's going to do it  and for us we happen to use a two week  Sprint time but you could use a  different amount of time as well  so what I'm going to do next is show you  what I spent our Sprint boat looks like  to give you a sense of that  so we have five columns features not  started the client in progress and done  in the feature column you're going to  have a overarching project so for  example preparing for this R Studio  conference talk  and within that you're going to have a  number of tasks to complete within your  Sprint  so in the not started I might have tasks  about drafting slides or practicing this  presentation and you can view the judge  of whether I successfully did these or  not  I may have decline columns such as  adding cat gifts and at some point  realized or decided this wasn't a  professional enough thing to add so I'm  not going to do that  I'll have it in progress column such as  drafting outline which I need to do  prior to doing the slides or practicing  my presentation  and then lastly I'll finish a couple of  tasks too so such as making this Sprint  board example which again you can judge  whether I successfully did that or not  and then we'll have other rows with  other features for separate tasks and  these this creates a really great  visualization for a team of how we're  doing in the Sprint  and what's really powerful is that as  Sprints continue to occur we can look  back in time and see how successful  we're at completing our task and modify  how we were going to move forward in the  next Sprint  and if you're disappointed that I didn't  add any cat gives I added some last  minute for you  so next how did we improve our  collaboration so when I first started we  were using git and devops principles in  some places but it wasn't a main part of  our workflow so a lot of our work was  shared across a shared drive or folder  in at work and that often meant copying  your our script for example and then  making edits and later putting it back  together which over time we're starting  to make a lot of problems especially  when it came down to production or it  was late at night at midnight and edits  are just very frustrating and tiring to  do  um so we started to use get principle  get or get workflow more effectively and  made it a main part of our workflow  so if you think about our work in our  studio workbench it's kind of like we're  in a silo it's really hard to share this  work with others or the colleagues and  we want to be able to do that  so that's where we started using  repositories via zero devops and a  repository is a slice a place where you  can store your code safely have Version  Control and it's the central place that  all of your your work can live  and what's great about this is that via  our studio and get we can move our work  outside of our work outside of our Silo  into this repository and that same vein  I can move other people's work from the  repositories into my workbench and work  on it there  now you can have an entire Workshop  about git and still may not understand  it it's very confusing but it's a really  great resource by Jenny Bryan called  happygit that's related to using git and  R that I highly encourage you to check  out if you want to get started with  getting r  so you can think of repositories as if  you're storing your code safely so in  these photos by Edward Newbridge we're  seeing this the spirit in flight and  we're getting snapshots snapshots of it  over time and you can think of our code  every time we make a commit or a save  we're saving a version of it in memory  in a repository  so maybe you want to put this last  snapshot into production but something  goes wrong  what's great about repository is that we  can roll back the clock and use this  previous version to go into production  and in the meantime fix our errors that  occurred later on  another another really great feature is  that you can make what's called a fork  or a branch repository and that's like  making a copy of it and you can kind of  think of it as if you're working in your  own lane in that sense we're all running  in the Sprint and I have my own fork or  branch and I'm working it making edits  and if I happen to make a mistake it's  not going to affect others in their Lane  so it gives us a really great  opportunity to try new things out fall  over break the app all those things that  we don't want to happen during  production  and lastly how did we improve our  turnaround time and scalability so we're  always getting requests and lots of data  that we're taking ownership of and these  data processing can take hours  and at the same time we also want to be  able to scale our work and share it with  others  so that's where our final product comes  in our studio connect and this is a  server that allows you to automate your  processes and what's really cool is that  from a repositories and Azure devops we  can deploy our work into our studio  connect  and from connect we can automate data  processing we can schedule reports  and we can post shiny apps  so RCA connect provided us more  processing power which allowed us to do  analyzes more quickly so just as an  example this is one of our servers we  have about 251 gigabytes of RAM which is  a lot or another example the amount of  CPU queries available and we have a  number of these servers available to us  to do different types of data processing  or host apps and I can can't explain the  giddiness I felt the first time I ran a  script on connect that took 10 minutes  when it used to take over an hour to run  if it didn't crash so connect was a  really great life saver in terms of  finishing or data processing in more  quickly quick manner allowing me to go  to sleep earlier  so it's not only quicker but it also  feed up our hands at least in our  environment we're limited to two  workbenches so it's kind of like having  two our consoles or two tabs and if I  have many types of data that I need to  process throughout the week it's as if  my hands are tied so by being able to  offset it off to a server I can free up  my hands to do other type of work  and we can schedule jobs on the server  I'm using for example the officer or  open xlsx package to make PowerPoints  Excel sheets or Word Documents  um in that same theater processing we  can also be doing the data the large  amount of data processing and then  creating reports that are outputted at a  scheduled time  and we can schedule emails with the  Blaster package and a couple emails here  and there is not too bad but when we  start to you need to share your emails  and data with a lot of different  partners this becomes a really great  tool to use  and here's one example of data that we  put on the website weekly for post  vaccination infection data if you want  to check it out and all of this data is  processed via connect and then shared  with our partners at cdpage to later  publish on the website  so we can kind of think of connect as if  they're replacing this they're robots  they're running in their own lane and  they're running their own processes  and it's at this point I might want to  sit back and look at my cat and listen  to her and stop looking at this monitor  but in reality automation isn't perfect  and it often actually fails and can be  quite challenging to debug I'm working  on a server the debugging process is a  lot different the logs are different so  we we tried to so we need to think about  that as well so in reality it's more  like this we're all running in the same  Lane some of the processes are falling  over I'm falling over Etc  and one of the solutions to this is was  to create a data processing monitoring  app so this monitors all of our  processes on connect and it gives us a  really great visualization of what's  occurring and when it might fail  and in addition to that we have an error  logging system where if something breaks  we get an email via blastula telling us  something's broken and where in that  script it broke so I don't have time to  share that with you right now but if  you're interested and there's actually a  talk by my colleagues Colby Parish Andy  fan coming up next that you can check  out to see that specific piece of our  workflow  and then lastly we have many different  chatting apps mostly internal that I  can't share with you but one of those  published to the public by the  California covid-19 modeling team called  Calcutt and this shows you the current  state and predicted feature State using  different models of covid-19 in the  state of California so encourage you to  check this out and see the different  types of data that we're working with  and what's I think really cool is that  you can download the data as well that  we've all been processing and that's  updated regularly  so this gives you a very high level  overview of the different tools that we  use to create this workflow using Azure  devops RSV workbench and our studio  connect allowing us to process data in a  very quick amount of time and freeing up  our hands to make products that help  guide decision making in the state of  where to allocate resources  so thank you all for your time and  special thanks to data processing and  informatics section for all our homework  [Applause]  [Music]

